# Deploy And Branches

Use this Rule for every git operation and every change that must reach the WordPress server in a SmartFlow workspace.

## Delivery Chain

Repository (wp-content level, private remote) -> user-confirmed local commit -> hard stop and hand-over -> the user pushes -> the project deploy path delivers only the child theme subdirectory -> the status bridge confirms the deployed commit -> served QA.

`docs/`, `.cursor/`, `.env`, `tmp/`, and other repo-level files never reach the server.

## The Agent Never Pushes

- No `git push`, no remote branch creation or deletion, no force operations, no remote tags, under any circumstances.
- After committing server-relevant work: hard stop. Hand over with the commit hash and a short statement of what the deploy will deliver. The user pushes; the deploy path delivers the child theme.
- After the user reports pushing, verify the deploy over the status bridge (`deployed_commit` against local `git rev-parse HEAD`) with the bounded retry and abort budget from the `status-bridge` Rule. Never record a served or final QA pass while the hashes differ.

## Bundle Deploy Phases

Everything that needs a deploy goes into one pass: WST templates and partials, ACF JSON field groups (with pull-before-deploy per the `acf-local-json` Rule), bridge updates, tracked CSS. Then one commit and one hard stop. After the deploy is verified, iterate locally with injection-proof QA instead of producing per-tweak deploy rounds; the next deploy pass collects the resulting tracked changes.

## Branches

- Work on the branch recorded in `PROJECT-CONTEXT.md`; stay on the default working branch unless told otherwise.
- Do not create, switch, merge, or rebase branches without an explicit user instruction.
- Never rewrite published history.

## Protected Branches

`main`, `master`, and `trunk` are write-protected for the agent. In many projects, servers deploy these branches automatically — any write there is effectively a deploy and belongs to the user alone.

Forbidden on `main`/`master`/`trunk`, in addition to the global push ban:

- `git commit` (no commits directly on these branches)
- `git merge` (nothing gets merged into these branches)
- `git revert` (reversals only through the user's own workflow after an explicit user request)

Read operations (`git log`, `git diff`, `git show`, checkout for inspection) stay allowed. Commits on the recorded working branch are the normal flow. If `PROJECT-CONTEXT.md` records `main`, `master`, or `trunk` as the working branch, stop and have the user resolve the contradiction before committing anything.

## Subagents

When delegating to subagents, put these prohibitions explicitly into the worker prompt. No worker prompt may allow a push, a commit, or a merge into `main`, `master`, or `trunk`, or force one indirectly through verification requirements (for example a served or bridge-verified check that only the user's push can satisfy). Commits stay in the main chat and always pass the confirmation gate below. Deploy steps end for every agent with "committed on the working branch, hand-over to the user".

## Commits

**The agent never commits on its own.** Every commit needs an explicit user confirmation in this session, given immediately before the commit happens:

- When a work step is complete and consistent, **prepare** the commit: name the files to be staged, summarize the change, and propose the commit message. Then stop and ask for the go.
- Only an explicit yes to that proposal authorizes exactly that one commit. A general task instruction ("bau die Section", "fix das Ticket") is never commit authorization, and one confirmation never covers future commits. The single exception is a scoped standing mandate that a workflow Skill explicitly asks for and the user explicitly grants (for example the per-ticket commit mandate of `jira-batch-workflow`) — it covers only the commits that Skill names, only for that run.
- On no, or on requested changes: adjust and re-propose. Never commit to checkpoint progress, "to be safe", or because a Skill step says a commit is due — the Skill defines **when** a commit is due, the user decides **that** it happens.
- Keep commits cohesive instead of committing fragments; apply the SmartFlow commit trailer per the `commit-trailer` Rule.
